Dot this with the first row $\bm r_1=(A_{11},A_{12},A_{13})$, component by component:
\begin{align*}
\bm r_1\cdot(\bm r_2\times\bm r_3)&=A_{11}(A_{22}A_{33}-A_{23}A_{32})+A_{12}(A_{23}A_{31}-A_{21}A_{33})+A_{13}(A_{21}A_{32}-A_{22}A_{31})\\
&=A_{11}(A_{22}A_{33}-A_{23}A_{32})-A_{12}(A_{21}A_{33}-A_{23}A_{31})+A_{13}(A_{21}A_{32}-A_{22}A_{31}),
\end{align*}
where the second line only rewrites the middle bracket as $A_{23}A_{31}-A_{21}A_{33}=-(A_{21}A_{33}-A_{23}A_{31})$. This is exactly \cref{eq:linear-algebra-det3}, so $\det A=\bm r_1\cdot(\bm r_2\times\bm r_3)$. Now recall the geometry of the triple product from first-year physics \cite{hrw}: $\abs{\bm r_2\times\bm r_3}$ is the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\bm r_2,\bm r_3$, and the cross product points perpendicular to it; dotting with $\bm r_1$ multiplies that area by the component of $\bm r_1$ along the perpendicular, which is the height of the parallelepiped (up to sign). Area of base times height is volume, so $\abs{\det A}=\abs{\bm r_1\cdot(\bm r_2\times\bm r_3)}$ is the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the rows.
